From 7e9c91f887bc532c4a558279cf89f21f518bfa95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 078/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: assemble FBC bodies from
 pooled chunks

In MMU mode the hardware reaches the compressed body only through the
per-frame page map - the vendor driver marks these pictures
scatter_alloc and never gives the body a base address at all (mc_y_adr
is left unassigned when mmu_enable is set, so HEVC_CM_BODY_START_ADDR
receives nothing meaningful).  The body therefore does not need to be
physically contiguous, but this driver was allocating it as one
dma_alloc_coherent per capture buffer: 16 MiB for a 4K 10-bit frame,
times a 24-buffer DPB.

Those 16 MiB contiguous requests were the single biggest source of CMA
pressure on the platform, failing with -EBUSY on a fragmented pool long
before the pool was actually full.

Assemble the body from fixed 1 MiB chunks handed out by a shared pool
instead, and build the frame map from them.  Chunks are fungible
between sessions regardless of resolution or bit depth, so the pool
absorbs geometry changes that previously forced a full free-and-realloc
cycle.  The non-MMU FBC path (pre-G12A) still allocates contiguously,
because there the hardware strides through the body directly.

Display safety is unchanged and still generational: a chunk released by
a session is never handed straight back out, because the VD1 plane may
still be scanning that frame.  Under memory pressure the parked
generations are recycled rather than failing the session, as before.

Two things the chunking makes possible that the old allocator could
not do:

 - the page map is now filled from the chunk list, and sized from the
   body alone (DIV_ROUND_UP(body_size, PAGE_SIZE)), matching the
   vendor's hevc_mmu_page_num() rather than over-counting the
   64K-aligned body+header total;

 - chunks that are neither in use nor parked are returned to the
   allocator at session stop.  The previous scheme kept whole parked
   buffers until a generation rotated out, which left the decoder
   squatting on its high-water mark: a 4K H.264 session starting right
   after an HEVC one would fail CAPTURE STREAMON with EINVAL because it
   could not get its own ~288 MB.  That now succeeds.

Validated on a VIM3 (G12B): HEVC Main10 PSNR 61.98 dB vs software
(gate 40); AM21C zero-copy 4K on the VD1 overlay as YU10 +
MOD_AMLOGIC_FBC(SCATTER) with no client errors; VP9 and H.264 1080p and
4K frame-md5 exact vs software; eight back-to-back 4K Main10 sessions
with zero decode errors and no progressive CMA decline; a 4K H.264
session immediately after those sessions is frame-md5 exact.
